/* data/learn-c/phy-12-04.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-04.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-04"] = {"4.1":{"s":"Thermodynamics studies heat, temperature and how heat and work turn into each other. Heat is a form of energy: doing work (like boring a cannon) can produce heat, and heat can do work (like an engine). This chapter builds the laws that govern these changes and applies them to processes, heat engines and refrigerators."},"4.2":{"s":"When a hotter and a colder body touch, heat flows from the hotter to the colder until there is no more net flow. They are then in thermal equilibrium, and they are at the same temperature.","w":["Equilibrium means no NET heat flow; it does not mean the two bodies hold the same amount of heat."]},"4.3":{"s":"Zeroth law: suppose A and B are each in thermal equilibrium with C. Then A and B are also in equilibrium with one another. This is what makes a thermometer work: the thermometer is the third system, so temperature becomes something we can measure and compare.","w":["It is called 'zeroth' because it was stated after the first and second laws, but it is more basic than both."]},"4.4":{"s":"Internal energy U is the total energy of the random motion (and interaction) of a system's molecules; for an ideal gas it depends only on temperature. A system can be open (exchanges energy and matter), closed (energy only) or isolated (neither). Heat Q is energy moving because of a temperature difference. Q is positive when the system gains heat and negative when it loses heat.","f":[{"x":"E_av = (3/2)*k*T","n":"Average energy per atom of a monatomic ideal gas such as argon (the internal energy is N times this); for a diatomic gas such as oxygen, (5/2)kT.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.4, p.77"}],"w":["A glass of water at rest still has internal energy: it is the molecules' random motion, not the motion of the whole glass."]},"4.5":{"s":"First law of thermodynamics: the heat given to a system goes partly into raising its internal energy and partly into work done by the system. It is the law of conservation of energy for systems that exchange heat and work. Work done by a gas pushing a piston out is positive; work done on the gas is negative.","f":[{"x":"Delta U = Q - W","n":"First law; Q = heat given to the system, W = work done BY the system.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.5, p.81"},{"x":"Q = Delta U + W","n":"The same law written for the heat.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.5, p.81"},{"x":"W = p*(V_f - V_i)","n":"Work done by a gas expanding at constant pressure p.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.5, p.81"}],"w":["Sign rule: Q positive when heat goes in; W positive when the system does work. Work done ON the gas is negative.","Use pressure in Pa and volume in m³ to get work in joules (1 L = 10⁻³ m³)."]},"4.6":{"s":"In thermodynamic equilibrium a system is in mechanical (same pressure throughout), chemical (no reactions or diffusion) and thermal (same temperature) equilibrium at once. Then state variables such as p, V and T describe it fully, and an equation of state links them. Intensive variables (p, T, density) do not depend on size; extensive ones (mass, U) do. On a p-V diagram, the area under the curve is the work done.","f":[{"x":"p*V = n*R*T","n":"Equation of state of an ideal gas; n = number of moles.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.6, p.84"},{"x":"W = P*(V_2 - V_1)","n":"Work at constant pressure: the rectangle under a horizontal p-V line. In general W = area under the p-V curve.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.6, p.85"}],"w":["A state that is not in equilibrium (a bursting balloon) cannot be shown as a point on a p-V diagram."]},"4.7":{"s":"A thermodynamic process takes a system from one state to another. The work done and heat added depend on the path, not only on the end states. Reversible processes can be run backwards exactly; real ones are irreversible because of friction and sudden changes. Main types: isothermal (T constant), isobaric (p constant), isochoric (V constant), adiabatic (no heat exchange), cyclic (returns to the start, so ΔU = 0) and free expansion (Q = W = ΔU = 0).","f":[{"x":"W = n*R*T*ln(V_f/V_i)","n":"Isothermal work by an ideal gas; here Delta U = 0, so Q = W.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.7, p.90"},{"x":"W = n*R*(T_f - T_i)","n":"Isobaric work by n moles of ideal gas (= p Delta V).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.7, p.91"},{"x":"C_p = C_v + R","n":"Molar specific heats of an ideal gas; isobaric heat Q = n C_p Delta T.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.7, p.91"},{"x":"Q = Delta U = n*C_v*Delta T","n":"Isochoric process: W = 0, all the heat raises the internal energy.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.7, p.92"},{"x":"p*V^gamma = constant","n":"Adiabatic process; gamma = C_p/C_v = 5/3 (monatomic), 7/5 (diatomic), 4/3 (triatomic).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.7, p.93"},{"x":"W = (p_f*V_f - p_i*V_i)/(1 - gamma)","n":"Adiabatic work by the gas; Q = 0, so Delta U = -W.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.7, p.93"}],"w":["Adiabatic expansion cools the gas, compression heats it, even though no heat flows.","The adiabatic p-V curve is steeper than the isothermal one through the same point."]},"4.8":{"s":"A heat engine takes heat from a hot reservoir, turns part of it into work and rejects the rest to a cold reservoir, over and over in a cycle. It needs a working substance, a hot source and a cold sink. Some heat is always rejected, so the efficiency is less than 1. On a p-V diagram the area enclosed by the cycle is the work per cycle.","f":[{"x":"W = |Q_H| - |Q_C|","n":"Net work per cycle (Delta U = 0 for a full cycle).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.8, p.97"},{"x":"eta = W/Q_H = 1 - |Q_C|/|Q_H|","n":"Thermal efficiency of a heat engine.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.8, p.98"}],"w":["Efficiency has no unit, but the work and both heats must be in the same unit."]},"4.9":{"s":"A refrigerator is a heat engine run backwards: work done by a compressor moves heat from a cold region to a hot one. The refrigerant evaporates in the cold coils (taking heat in) and condenses in the hot coils outside (giving heat out). An air conditioner works the same way for a room. A heat pump uses the same cycle to warm a building by taking heat from the cold air outside.","f":[{"x":"|Q_H| = |Q_C| + |W|","n":"Heat given to the hot side = heat taken from the cold side + work input.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.9, p.100"},{"x":"K = |Q_C|/|W| = |Q_C|/(|Q_H| - |Q_C|)","n":"Coefficient of performance of a refrigerator (about 5 for a household one).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.9, p.101"}],"w":["K can be larger than 1; it is not an efficiency.","Opening a fridge door in a closed room warms the room: more heat goes out the back than comes from inside."]},"4.10":{"s":"The first law allows things that never happen, such as heat flowing by itself from cold to hot, or all heat turning into work. The second law rules them out. Kelvin-Planck (engine) form: no engine can take heat from a hot reservoir and turn all of it into work; some must go to a cold reservoir. Clausius (refrigerator) form: heat cannot flow from a colder to a hotter body unless work is done.","w":["So no heat engine is 100% efficient and no refrigerator works without work input."]},"4.11":{"s":"The Carnot engine is an ideal reversible engine. Its cycle has two isothermal steps (taking in heat at the hot temperature, rejecting heat at the cold one) and two adiabatic steps. Its efficiency depends only on the two reservoir temperatures. No engine between the same two temperatures can beat it, whatever its working substance. Run backwards it is the Carnot refrigerator, which has the highest coefficient of performance.","f":[{"x":"eta = 1 - T_C/T_H","n":"Carnot efficiency; temperatures in kelvin.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.11, p.104"},{"x":"K = T_C/(T_H - T_C)","n":"Coefficient of performance of a Carnot refrigerator.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 4.11, p.105"}],"w":["Always use kelvin: 27 °C = 300 K.","Efficiency could be 100% only with the cold reservoir at 0 K, which cannot be reached."]},"4.12":{"s":"The Stirling cycle (spelt 'Sterling' in the book) is a closed cycle used in Stirling engines with air, helium or hydrogen as the working gas. It has two isothermal steps, one hot and one cold, joined by two steps at constant volume, drawn as vertical lines on the p-V diagram. Run in reverse it works as a refrigerator, used in cryogenics to reach very low temperatures.","w":["The book's text calls the joining steps 'isobaric' in places, but its figure draws them as vertical lines: in the Stirling cycle they are at constant volume."]}};
